  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong lower-layer carriage fit: the envelope keeps payloads opaque, binds interpretation to pCID dispatch, requires a signature/proof slot, and makes unsupported pCIDs fail explicitly. That lets Bob store and relay encrypted condition references without speculative parsing. The main gap is that release-condition meaning, satisfaction proofs, and peer-local promise accounting all live above the envelope and are not defined here.",
    "strengths": [
      "Unknown-pCID policy explicitly allows exact-byte store/forward while forbidding speculative parsing.",
      "Mandatory sig_pcid/sig_payload gives every envelope a named proof path and canonical bytes to verify.",
      "Layering rules clearly delegate condition semantics to the payload protocol instead of smearing them across lower layers."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not define the condition object, geofencing semantics, or how release authorization is proven.",
      "It does not state how Bob records \"opaque carriage only\" versus a stronger promise or acceptance of condition semantics.",
      "The spec is still draft and not frozen, so long-term handler/pCID stability is not yet demonstrated."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Opaque carriage can hide unsafe or unverifiable condition semantics unless upper layers make non-acceptance and verification duties explicit.",
      "Future auditors may have the bytes but still lack the pCID or sig_pCID handlers needed to evaluate the condition object."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What peer-local record proves Bob only stored or forwarded the envelope and did not promise semantic validation of the condition?",
      "How is a geofencing or release-condition object discovered and verified over time when only lower layers understand the outer envelope?",
      "What preservation plan keeps pCID and sig_pCID handler specs understandable on a 100-year horizon?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
